<div class="callout">
<div class="callout-icon">💡</div>
<div class="callout-text"><strong>Important context:</strong> Most publicly cited NBA vertical jump numbers are max verticals from pre-draft combine testing — not in-season measurements. An NBA player's in-game vertical at the end of a back-to-back is significantly lower than their fresh combine max. The numbers you see quoted online almost always refer to peak combine testing conditions.</div>
</div>
<h2>NBA Vertical Jump by Position</h2>
<p>Position plays a major role in vertical jump averages. Guards and wings — who are selected heavily for athleticism — tend to jump significantly higher than big men, who are selected more for size and skill.</p>
